# Overview
#
# A buzzword reads as technical but names nothing that a plainer word
# does not (`the bedrock of the design`). The reader must translate it
# back into the plain word. The fix writes the concrete word (`what the
# design depends on`). The rule sits at Recommended, because the writer
# must find the concrete word, so the fix is not mechanical.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` lists each buzzword. Several words count only inside their
# metaphor phrase, such as `bedrock of`, `nexus between`, and `paradigm
# shift`. `north star`, `gold plating`, and `gold plated` each match the
# hyphenated and the spaced spelling. A space in a pattern is a
# whitespace token, so the guard on `north star` sits glued to `star`
# and reads the token right after it. That guard skips `metric` and
# `metrics`.
#
# False Positives
#
# A word counts only inside its metaphor phrase when a product or a
# field uses the bare word. Weir ignores case, and Harper's
# part-of-speech tagger never marks `Bedrock` or `Nexus` as a proper
# noun, a name that takes a capital letter, such as `Kubernetes`. The
# phrase is therefore the only guard against AWS Bedrock and Sonatype
# Nexus. `bedrock for` needs `the`, because a sentence can pass
# something to the product (`hand the response to Amazon Bedrock for
# processing`).
#
# `paradigm` counts only as `paradigm shift` or `new paradigm`, because
# `programming paradigm` is a term of art. `north star` passes before
# `metric`, because `north star metric` names a product metric.
#
# `modality` is left out, because machine-learning docs use it for an
# input type (`an image modality`). `vantage` names a cost tool, and
# `locus` is a term in genetics and in math, so both stay out too. Other
# words stay out because each names something in code or operations.
# `vector` names a math type, `primitive` names a language built-in, and
# `surface` names an attack surface. `harness` names a test harness, and
# `scaffolding` names a code generator. `ratchet` names a lint baseline
# that only tightens, and `evacuate` names a node drain.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit no lines in the measure.py corpus, so no sample measures
# its precision.
expr main [substrate, substrates, endgame, flywheel, flywheels, (bedrock of), (the bedrock for), (nexus [of, between]), (paradigm shift), (new paradigm), (north[-, ( )]star!(( )[metric, metrics])), (gold[-, ( )]plating), (gold[-, ( )]plated), (wedge in)]
let message "Buzzword. Use the concrete word."
let description "Flags buzzwords that stand in for a concrete term."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Deploy the model on Amazon Bedrock in us-east-1."
allows "The response goes back to Amazon Bedrock for further processing."
allows "Push the artifact to Nexus before the release job runs."
allows "The model accepts an image modality and a text modality."
allows "Go supports more than one programming paradigm."
allows "Our north star metric is weekly active users."
